From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: an Electron page with Node.js gets its own renderer

An Electron app's window with Node.js in its page (nodeIntegration, or
sandbox: false) runs its preload, and with integration its page, with
Node. Electron starts such a page's renderer unsandboxed and outside
the zygote, as only that renderer runs Node. This does the same.

- ContentBrowserClient::MainFrameNavigatesInto: the embedder learns the
  SiteInstance an outermost main frame's navigation goes to before its
  renderer starts, as the frame for it may still be speculative (Electron's
  RegisterPendingSiteInstance).
- A renderer whose command line has --no-zygote launches without the
  zygote (Electron's support_mixed_sandbox_with_zygote.patch), and that
  renderer alone may start setuid programs, as Node's child_process does in Electron
  (allow_new_privileges_in_unsandboxed_child_processes.patch).
- ChromeContentBrowserClient: the renderer a Node page's navigation
  gets (SiteInstanceGotProcessAndSite, when the SiteInstance had none
  yet) is launched with --no-sandbox, --no-zygote and Domicile's Node
  switch, takes no spare and no other existing renderer
  (IsSuitableHost), which started sandboxed, and is never reused for
  another page (MayReuseHost). See chrome/browser/domicile/domicile_electron_page.h.

Verified: not built. This repository cannot build Chromium; the engine
job builds it on crux.

Co-Authored-By: Connor Prussin <connor@prussin.net>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
